# Plan meaningful global themes

Read the complete numbered collection.
Identify up to maximum_groups distinct recurring semantic themes that together cover it.
Use fewer groups when there are fewer genuinely distinct themes; never force trivial splits.
This call plans categories only: do not assign item identifiers, repeat members or attempt to reconstruct the list.

Return JSON groups. For each group, give a short label within max_length characters and a concise scope explaining which main meanings belong there, including a boundary against the most similar neighboring theme.
Use the actual meanings across the collection, not source order, demographic stereotypes, stylistic differences or a universal source-domain label.

The same fixed groups and scopes will be used to assign bounded batches of the original items.
Make them specific enough to be useful and broad enough to cover every supplied item.
Mixed items will go to their best-fitting main theme. No category should mean merely miscellaneous leftovers.
Do not prescribe desired beliefs or rewrite the supplied statements.

Return only the requested global theme definitions.
There is no need to reason through or list thousands of individual assignments in this planning call.
